  // ---------------- INDUCTION AGENTS ----------------
  addDrug({
    id: "propofol", cat: "induction", name: "Propofol", brand: "Diprivan",
    classification: "Alkylphenol (2,6-diisopropylphenol) • GABA-A receptor agonist",
    tagline: "The default IV induction agent almost everywhere — fast on, fast off, and pleasant to wake up from",
    tags: ["GABA-A agonist", "TIVA"],
    source: SRC.fdaUpToDate("Diprivan (propofol) injectable emulsion label", "Fresenius Kabi/AstraZeneca"),
    structure: "Propofol is 2,6-diisopropylphenol — chemically, just a phenol ring with two bulky isopropyl groups hanging off it. That simple structure makes it intensely lipophilic and essentially insoluble in water, which is why it can't be dissolved in a normal saline-type vehicle the way most IV drugs are. Instead it's suspended as a white, milky oil-in-water emulsion — soybean oil, glycerol and egg lecithin — the same reason it stings on the way in (the drug itself irritates the vein) and why an opened vial is a genuine infection risk if it isn't handled with strict aseptic technique: that lipid emulsion is a perfectly good culture medium for bacteria.",
    pd: "Its main job is potentiating the GABA-A receptor — it increases how long the receptor's chloride channel stays open in response to GABA, hyperpolarising neurons and damping down the whole CNS. At higher concentrations it also dampens NMDA receptor activity, which contributes to the depth of unconsciousness it produces. Clinically that translates into reliable hypnosis and amnesia, a genuinely useful antiemetic effect (patients who wake up on propofol are far less nauseated than after a volatile-only anaesthetic), and some anticonvulsant activity. The trade-off is real and dose-dependent: it drops blood pressure by both dilating vessels and mildly depressing the myocardium, and it depresses breathing enough to cause outright apnoea at induction doses — which is exactly why you always have airway equipment ready before you push it.",
    pk: "Onset is about one arm-to-brain circulation time — roughly 30 seconds — because it's so lipid-soluble that it crosses into the brain almost as fast as it reaches it. What makes it special for maintenance infusions is how it behaves afterward: it redistributes out of the brain into muscle and fat quickly, so a single dose wears off fast, and even after a multi-hour infusion its context-sensitive half-time stays comparatively short, so patients still wake up promptly. The liver does almost all the work of clearing it (glucuronidation and sulfation to inactive metabolites), with clearance so high it actually exceeds hepatic blood flow — a sign that some metabolism is happening outside the liver too, in the lungs and kidneys.",
    dosage: "FDA-approved: for induction in a healthy adult under 55, 2–2.5 mg/kg IV, given in small increments (about 40 mg every 10 seconds) rather than as one push — go slower and use less in the elderly, the debilitated, or anyone ASA III/IV, since they tolerate the blood pressure drop far less well. For maintenance, 100–200 mcg/kg/min as an infusion, titrated to effect. For monitored sedation outside the OR, start lower still — 25–75 mcg/kg/min, or a slow 0.5 mg/kg bolus over 3–5 minutes. In a ventilated ICU patient, start at 5 mcg/kg/min and titrate up in small steps rather than loading quickly.",
    offLabel: "A sub-hypnotic dose (10–20 mg IV) is a well-known rescue treatment for PONV when standard antiemetics haven't worked. It's also used, again off-label, for procedural sedation by trained non-anaesthesia staff under many institutional protocols, and as a continuous infusion for refractory status epilepticus when first-line agents have failed.",
    complications: "The two things that will bite you fastest are hypotension and apnoea — both dose-dependent, both expected, both why you never give propofol without full monitoring and a plan for the airway. Injection pain is common and can be blunted by giving it in a large vein or mixing in a little lidocaine first. The one complication worth knowing cold, even though it's rare, is Propofol Infusion Syndrome — metabolic acidosis, rhabdomyolysis, hyperkalaemia and cardiac failure from prolonged high-dose infusion, seen more in children, in critically ill patients, and with very high infusion rates sustained for days rather than hours. And because it's a lipid emulsion, strict aseptic handling matters more here than with almost any other anaesthetic drug — an opened vial left standing is a genuine bacterial growth risk, not just a theoretical one.",
    references: [
      "FDA Prescribing Information — Diprivan (propofol injectable emulsion, Fresenius Kabi/AstraZeneca); UpToDate (2025/2026).",
      "Miller's Anesthesia, 10th ed., Ch. 21 (Intravenous Anesthetics); Stoelting's Pharmacology & Physiology, 5th ed.",
      "Kam PC, Cardone D. Propofol infusion syndrome. Anaesthesia (2007)."
    ]
  });

  addDrug({
    id: "etomidate", cat: "induction", name: "Etomidate", brand: "Amidate",
    classification: "Carboxylated imidazole ester • Positive allosteric GABA-A modulator",
    tagline: "The induction agent you reach for when the heart can't afford much of a hit",
    tags: ["Cardiac-stable induction", "Adrenal suppression"],
    source: SRC.fdaUpToDate("Amidate (etomidate) injection label", "Hospira/Pfizer"),
    structure: "Etomidate is an imidazole-containing compound, structurally unrelated to propofol, barbiturates, or any other induction agent — it sits in a chemical family of its own. The imidazole ring gives it a useful trick: it's water-soluble at acidic pH but converts to a more lipid-soluble, CNS-active form once it hits the body's physiological pH after injection. It's formulated in propylene glycol rather than a lipid emulsion, which is part of why it stings going in and can cause superficial phlebitis at the injection site.",
    pd: "Like propofol, it works mainly through the GABA-A receptor, producing hypnosis with essentially no analgesia of its own. What actually sets it apart clinically is what it doesn't do to the cardiovascular system — heart rate, blood pressure, and contractility barely move after an induction dose, which is precisely why it's the go-to choice for a patient whose heart has very little reserve to spare. It also lowers cerebral metabolic rate and intracranial pressure while keeping cerebral perfusion pressure intact, useful in neuro cases. The trade-off that limits its use everywhere else is adrenal suppression: even a single induction dose measurably and reversibly inhibits 11β-hydroxylase, the enzyme the adrenal cortex needs to make cortisol, for several hours afterward.",
    pk: "Onset is fast — 30 to 60 seconds — and a single dose wears off quickly too, through rapid redistribution out of the brain, the same mechanism that gives propofol its short duration. The catch is that this redistribution-driven offset only works well for a single bolus; give it repeatedly or run it as an infusion and the context-sensitive half-time climbs sharply, which combined with the adrenal suppression risk is exactly why etomidate is essentially never used for maintenance, only for induction. It's cleared by a mix of hepatic and plasma esterase hydrolysis, with about three-quarters of it protein-bound in the blood.",
    dosage: "FDA-approved: 0.2–0.6 mg/kg IV as a single induction dose, given over 30–60 seconds — most adults land around 0.3 mg/kg. The label doesn't cover it for maintenance infusion or repeat dosing, and that's a real restriction, not just a formality, given the adrenal suppression risk with repeated exposure.",
    offLabel: "It's sometimes chosen, carefully and with the debate around it acknowledged, as a single induction dose for rapid sequence induction in septic or otherwise critically ill patients specifically because of its haemodynamic stability — the argument being that even a single-dose adrenal effect is a smaller risk than a hypotensive crash at induction in someone with almost no cardiovascular reserve. It also sees use as an induction agent in high-risk cardiac patients undergoing procedural sedation where blood pressure swings are poorly tolerated.",
    complications: "Adrenal suppression is the headline issue — real, measurable, and the reason many units now give empiric stress-dose steroids after using etomidate in critically ill or septic patients, even though the single-dose clinical significance is still debated in the literature. Myoclonus is common and can look alarming — it's disinhibition of subcortical structures, not a seizure, and it's blunted by pre-treating with an opioid or benzodiazepine. Pain on injection and superficial phlebitis are common. PONV is more frequent than with propofol. On the plus side, it causes little histamine release and comparatively little respiratory depression, though apnoea can still happen, especially with concurrent opioids.",
    references: [
      "FDA Prescribing Information — Amidate (etomidate injection, Hospira/Pfizer); UpToDate (2025/2026).",
      "Miller's Anesthesia, 10th ed., Ch. 21; Stoelting's Pharmacology & Physiology, 5th ed.",
      "Albert SG, et al. The effect of etomidate on adrenal function in critical illness: a systematic review. Intensive Care Med."
    ]
  });

  addDrug({
    id: "ketamine", cat: "induction", name: "Ketamine", brand: "Ketalar",
    classification: "Arylcycloalkylamine (phencyclidine congener) • Non-competitive NMDA receptor antagonist",
    tagline: "The odd one out — it provides its own analgesia, keeps the patient breathing, and raises the blood pressure instead of dropping it",
    tags: ["NMDA antagonist", "Dissociative anaesthesia"],
    source: SRC.fdaUpToDate("Ketalar (ketamine hydrochloride) injection label", "Par Pharmaceutical"),
    structure: "Ketamine is an arylcycloalkylamine, chemically related to phencyclidine (PCP) — a lineage that explains some of its more unusual psychoactive effects. It's used clinically as a racemic mixture of its two mirror-image forms (R and S enantiomers); the S(+) form alone is also separately marketed (as esketamine) and is roughly twice as potent as the racemic mixture.",
    pd: "Almost everything distinctive about ketamine follows from one fact: it's a non-competitive antagonist at the NMDA receptor, not a GABA-A agonist like the other induction agents. That gives it a completely different clinical signature — a 'dissociative' state where the patient looks disconnected from their surroundings rather than simply unconscious, combined with profound analgesia (something none of the other induction agents provide at all) and, usefully, often-preserved airway reflexes, spontaneous breathing, and muscle tone. It's also the one induction agent that tends to raise heart rate and blood pressure rather than lower them, because it stimulates the sympathetic nervous system centrally and blocks catecholamine reuptake — though in a patient whose own catecholamine stores are already exhausted (severe shock, prolonged critical illness), its direct negative inotropic effect can be unmasked and it may drop blood pressure instead. It's a genuine bronchodilator too, which matters in anyone with reactive airway disease.",
    pk: "Onset is 30–60 seconds IV, 3–4 minutes IM — the intramuscular route being genuinely useful in an uncooperative or needle-phobic patient where IV access isn't yet possible. It's highly lipid-soluble, gets into the brain fast, and a single dose wears off quickly through redistribution. The liver breaks it down via CYP3A4 and CYP2B6 into norketamine, an active metabolite carrying roughly a fifth to a third of the parent drug's potency, which is then further metabolised and excreted renally.",
    dosage: "FDA-approved: for induction of general anaesthesia, 1–4.5 mg/kg IV (typically 1.5–2 mg/kg IV given over 60 seconds), or 6.5–13 mg/kg IM (typically 8–10 mg/kg IM) if IV access isn't available. Maintenance is achieved with repeat doses of 0.5 mg/kg IV as needed, or an infusion of 10–45 mcg/kg/min (0.6–2.7 mg/kg/h). Sub-Dissociative Acute Pain Analgesia (ASRA-PM/AAPM Guidelines): Preoperative or intraoperative IV bolus of 0.1–0.3 mg/kg IV, followed by a continuous low-dose infusion of 0.1–0.25 mg/kg/h (approximately 2–5 mcg/kg/min), continuing into PACU/ward. This sub-anesthetic regimen delivers powerful opioid-sparing analgesia and prevents opioid-induced hyperalgesia without causing psychotomimetic emergence phenomena.",
    offLabel: "At sub-dissociative doses (0.1–0.3 mg/kg IV or 0.1–0.25 mg/kg/h infusion), it is a cornerstone of multimodal perioperative opioid-sparing analgesia and treatment of central sensitization / opioid-tolerant surgical pain. It is also used for procedural sedation in emergency departments, as an induction agent of choice in status asthmaticus refractory to standard bronchodilators, and — as esketamine specifically — is FDA-approved in its own right for treatment-resistant depression. Intranasal (3–6 mg/kg) or oral (5–10 mg/kg) racemic ketamine is a well-established option for paediatric premedication or procedural sedation when IV access is absent.",
    complications: "Emergence phenomena — vivid dreams, hallucinations, a dysphoric 'coming back' experience — are the most talked-about downside, and they're meaningfully reduced by co-administering a benzodiazepine and by keeping the recovery environment quiet and undisturbed. It raises heart rate, blood pressure and myocardial oxygen demand, which makes it a poor choice in a patient with poorly controlled ischaemic heart disease or severe hypertension. It increases salivation enough that an antisialagogue is often given beforehand, and despite 'preserved' airway reflexes, laryngospasm from those secretions can still occur. It raises intraocular pressure. And because it has real recreational misuse potential, it's a Schedule III controlled substance in the US and needs to be tracked and stored accordingly.",
    references: [
      "FDA Prescribing Information — Ketalar (ketamine hydrochloride injection, Par Pharmaceutical); UpToDate (2025/2026); Miller's Anesthesia, 10th ed., Ch. 22.",
      "Schwenk ES, et al. Consensus Guidelines on the Use of Intravenous Ketamine Infusions for Acute Pain Management From the American Society of Regional Anesthesia and Pain Medicine, the American Academy of Pain Medicine, and the American Society of Anesthesiologists. Reg Anesth Pain Med (2018).",
      "Stoelting's Pharmacology & Physiology in Anesthetic Practice, 5th ed."
    ]
  });

  addDrug({
    id: "thiopental", cat: "induction", name: "Thiopental (Sodium Thiopental)", brand: "Pentothal",
    classification: "Thiobarbiturate (sulfur-substituted barbituric acid) • GABA-A channel potentiator",
    tagline: "The original rapid-acting induction agent — historically important, no longer available in the US",
    tags: ["Barbiturate", "Historic agent"],
    source: "FDA-approved prescribing information for Pentothal (thiopental sodium), last US-marketed formulation (Hospira) — product discontinued from the US market in 2011; UpToDate \"Barbiturates\" monograph (2025); Miller's Anesthesia, 10th ed., Ch. 21.",
    structure: "Thiopental is a thiobarbiturate — an oxybarbiturate with a sulfur atom swapped in for oxygen at one position on the barbituric-acid ring. That single substitution increases its lipid solubility and speeds up both its onset and its redistribution-driven offset compared with older oxybarbiturates. It's formulated as a strongly alkaline powder (pH 10–11) for reconstitution, and that alkalinity is genuinely dangerous if the injection goes wrong — extravasation or accidental intra-arterial injection causes severe tissue and vascular damage.",
    pd: "It potentiates GABA-A receptor chloride conductance, much like propofol, and at higher concentrations can activate the receptor directly even without GABA present. It has no analgesic effect of its own — in fact it can be mildly antanalgesic at low doses. It causes dose-dependent myocardial depression and venodilation (so hypotension is a real risk, especially in anyone hypovolaemic), and dose-dependent respiratory depression up to outright apnoea. On the positive side, it reduces cerebral metabolic rate, cerebral blood flow and intracranial pressure, which is why it was historically used for cerebral protection and to produce burst suppression.",
    pk: "Onset is about 30 seconds, and after a single dose it wears off quickly through redistribution — but here's the catch that eventually limited its use for anything beyond a single induction: its actual elimination half-life is long, over 10 hours, so repeated or prolonged dosing causes real accumulation and dramatically prolonged sedation, unlike propofol's more forgiving kinetics. The liver clears it, mainly via CYP-mediated oxidation.",
    dosage: "Historic FDA-approved dosing: 3–5 mg/kg IV for induction in a healthy adult, titrated, with lower doses in the elderly or hypovolaemic. It is no longer available in the US market — the sole remaining manufacturer, Hospira, discontinued it in 2011, for reasons tied to manufacturing and its use in lethal injection — though it's retained here because it still appears in some other countries' practice and remains a fixture of exam and historical teaching.",
    offLabel: "Where it's still available, it's occasionally used for status epilepticus refractory to first- and second-line agents, and for therapeutic burst suppression in refractory raised intracranial pressure — roles largely taken over by propofol elsewhere.",
    complications: "Extravasation or accidental intra-arterial injection causes severe tissue necrosis and thrombosis — historically managed by injecting local anaesthetic or a vasodilator into the same artery to limit the damage. It causes more histamine release than propofol, so laryngospasm and bronchospasm are more of a concern. It's absolutely contraindicated in acute intermittent porphyria — barbiturates induce ALA synthase and can trigger a life-threatening attack. And because of its long elimination half-life, repeated dosing causes genuinely prolonged recovery in a way that simply doesn't happen with propofol.",
    references: [
      "FDA-Approved Prescribing Information \u2014 Sodium Thiopental for Injection; US FDA Electronic Orange Book.",
      "UpToDate \"Drug Information: Thiopental\" (Wolters Kluwer, 2025/2026).",
      "Miller's Anesthesia, 10th ed. (Gropper MA, ed.), Ch. 26: Intravenous Anesthetics, Elsevier, 2025/2026.",
      "Stoelting's Pharmacology & Physiology in Anesthetic Practice, 5th ed., Ch. 4: Barbiturates, Wolters Kluwer."
    ]
  });

  addDrug({
    id: "midazolam", cat: "induction", name: "Midazolam", brand: "Versed",
    classification: "Water-soluble imidazobenzodiazepine • GABA-A positive allosteric modulator",
    tagline: "The benzodiazepine anaesthetists actually use — for calming nerves before a case, not usually for the induction itself",
    tags: ["Benzodiazepine", "GABA-A modulator"],
    source: SRC.fdaUpToDate("Versed (midazolam) injection label", "Pfizer"),
    structure: "Midazolam is an imidazobenzodiazepine. The imidazole ring is the clever part of the design — it keeps the molecule water-soluble at the acidic pH of the injectable formulation, then the ring closes up into a more lipophilic, CNS-penetrant form once it hits the body's physiological pH after injection. That's what gives it a faster onset than older benzodiazepines like diazepam, which have to rely purely on their intrinsic lipid solubility.",
    pd: "It's a positive allosteric modulator at the benzodiazepine site of the GABA-A receptor — rather than opening the chloride channel itself, it makes the channel open more often in response to GABA. Clinically that gives you sedation, anxiolysis, reliable anterograde amnesia (patients genuinely don't remember the unpleasant parts), and anticonvulsant activity, but no analgesia at all. At sedative doses it's remarkably haemodynamically gentle; at induction doses, or combined with an opioid, it can cause real hypotension. Respiratory depression is dose-dependent and becomes dramatically more pronounced when an opioid is on board too — that combination is one of the most common causes of sedation-related respiratory events in practice.",
    pk: "Onset is 1–3 minutes IV, with peak effect a little after that. Duration is short-to-intermediate but genuinely less predictable than propofol or etomidate, particularly in the elderly, the obese, or anyone with hepatic impairment. The liver clears it via CYP3A4 into 1-hydroxymidazolam, an active metabolite that's renally excreted and can accumulate in renal failure — worth remembering in a patient with poor kidney function who seems to be taking unusually long to wake up.",
    dosage: "FDA-approved: for premedication, 1–2 mg IV increments (or 0.07–0.08 mg/kg IM), titrated to effect. For procedural sedation, small increments of 0.5–2 mg IV, titrated, usually totalling under 5 mg. For induction of general anaesthesia, 0.1–0.3 mg/kg IV — less if the patient's already had opioid premedication, or if they're elderly or high-risk. For continuous ICU sedation, 0.02–0.1 mg/kg/h as an infusion.",
    offLabel: "Intranasal or buccal midazolam (roughly 0.2–0.3 mg/kg, maximum around 10 mg) is widely used, particularly in children, for anxiolytic premedication before a case when IV access isn't yet in place. A separate, FDA-approved intranasal product (Nayzilam) exists for a genuinely different indication — rescue treatment of intermittent, stereotypic seizure clusters — dosed as 5 mg (one spray) into one nostril, repeatable once after 10 minutes if needed. Intramuscular midazolam at the same premedication dose is a standard option for acute agitation. And it's a common component of continuous infusion regimens for refractory status epilepticus.",
    complications: "Respiratory depression is the big one, especially combined with opioids — always ask what else the patient has on board before you dose it. Paradoxical agitation happens, more often in children and the elderly, and can look confusingly like undersedation if you don't recognise it for what it is. Recovery can be genuinely prolonged in the elderly, the obese, anyone with hepatic impairment, or with a CYP3A4 inhibitor on board (certain antifungals and macrolides, for instance). Its anterograde amnesia is usually the whole point, but it's worth remembering it's happening if a patient seems oddly unbothered by something distressing. And if things do go too far, flumazenil is the specific reversal agent for benzodiazepine-induced sedation and respiratory depression.",
    references: [
      "FDA Prescribing Information — Versed (midazolam injection, Pfizer); UpToDate (2025/2026).",
      "Miller's Anesthesia, 10th ed., Ch. 21; Stoelting's Pharmacology & Physiology, 5th ed.",
      "ASA Practice Guidelines for Moderate Procedural Sedation and Analgesia (2018)."
    ]
  });

  addDrug({
    id: "remimazolam", cat: "induction", name: "Remimazolam", brand: "Byfavo",
    classification: "Ester-hydrolysed soft-drug benzodiazepine • Tissue carboxylesterase-cleared GABA-A modulator",
    tagline: "A benzodiazepine engineered to be broken down almost instantly — sedation you can turn off nearly as fast as you turned it on",
    tags: ["Benzodiazepine", "Ultra-short-acting", "Ester-metabolised"],
    source: "FDA-approved prescribing information — Byfavo (remimazolam besylate) injection label (Acacia Pharma/Cosette Pharmaceuticals), approved July 2, 2020; UpToDate \"Remimazolam: Drug information\" (2025); Miller's Anesthesia, 10th ed.",
    structure: "Remimazolam takes the familiar benzodiazepine core and does something genuinely clever with it: it grafts on a carboxylic ester side chain. That single addition is the whole point of the molecule — it turns midazolam's usual liver-dependent clearance into something completely different (see Pharmacokinetics below), and it's the same basic trick that made remifentanil such a distinctive opioid.",
    pd: "Pharmacodynamically it behaves like any other benzodiazepine — a positive allosteric modulator at the GABA-A receptor's benzodiazepine site, producing sedation, anxiolysis and amnesia without any analgesic effect of its own. What's different isn't what it does, it's how quickly it stops doing it, which is the entire clinical case for choosing it over midazolam. Haemodynamically it's gentle, similar to or slightly more stable than midazolam, and it causes the same class-wide dose-dependent respiratory depression, sharply worsened by concurrent opioids, that every benzodiazepine carries.",
    pk: "This is where remimazolam earns its keep. That ester side chain is rapidly hydrolysed by tissue carboxylesterases — an enzyme present throughout the body, not concentrated in the liver — into an essentially inactive metabolite. Practically, that means its clearance barely cares about renal function, hepatic function, age, body weight, sex or race (hepatic impairment is the one situation where its half-life is meaningfully prolonged, since the liver does still play some role). The result is a genuinely ultra-short duration of action, a short context-sensitive half-time even after repeated dosing, and — the feature that matters most at the bedside — a fast, predictable offset that midazolam, dependent on hepatic CYP3A4 metabolism, simply can't match.",
    dosage: "FDA-approved: for procedural sedation in adults undergoing a procedure lasting 30 minutes or less, an initial dose of 5 mg IV given over roughly 1 minute, followed by 2.5 mg top-up doses as needed, titrated to the desired level of sedation. It is not currently FDA-approved as a primary general anaesthesia induction agent — that use, seen in research and in some countries' broader clinical practice, sits outside its US label.",
    offLabel: "Its use as an induction and maintenance agent for general anaesthesia — sometimes combined with an opioid such as sufentanil — is an active area of research and adopted practice in some settings, exploiting the same fast, organ-independent offset that makes it attractive for procedural sedation, though this remains outside its current FDA-approved indication.",
    complications: "As a benzodiazepine, it carries the same class-wide risks as midazolam — dose-dependent respiratory depression, markedly potentiated by opioids, and hypotension, particularly in combination with other sedating or cardiodepressant drugs. Because its offset is so fast and so reliable, under-sedation or a patient rousing mid-procedure is arguably a more practical concern than prolonged sedation. It's a Schedule IV controlled substance in the US. And as with any newer agent, the safety experience — while reassuring so far — is simply smaller in volume than midazolam's decades of use, which is worth keeping in mind rather than assuming an identical risk profile purely because it's 'a benzodiazepine.'",
    references: [
      "FDA-Approved Prescribing Information \u2014 Byfavo (remimazolam) for injection, Acacia Pharma / Eagle Pharmaceuticals (approved 2020); US FDA.",
      "UpToDate \"Drug Information: Remimazolam\" (Wolters Kluwer, 2025/2026).",
      "European Medicines Agency (EMA) Summary of Product Characteristics \u2014 Byfavo (2021/2025).",
      "Miller's Anesthesia, 10th ed., Ch. 26: Intravenous Anesthetics \u2014 Benzodiazepines, Elsevier, 2025/2026."
    ]
  });

  addDrug({
    id: "cipepofol", cat: "induction", name: "Cipepofol (Ciprofol)", brand: "Cypsedo",
    classification: "Chiral cyclopropyl alkylphenol • High-potency GABA-A receptor agonist",
    tagline: "The newest FDA-approved general anaesthesia induction agent — a fluorinated propofol relative that works at a fraction of the dose",
    tags: ["GABA-A agonist", "Newest FDA approval"],
    source: "FDA-approved prescribing information — Cypsedo (cipepofol) injection label (Haisco Pharmaceutical Group / HaiscoUSA Pharmaceuticals), approved May 29, 2026; From propofol to cipepofol (Cypsedo®): Medicinal Chemistry Research (2026); UpToDate general anaesthesia induction agent updates (2026).",
    structure: "Cipepofol (also referred to in the literature as ciprofol) is a chiral analogue of propofol — take the propofol scaffold and add a cyclopropyl group engineered specifically to improve how stereospecifically the molecule binds the GABA-A receptor. It's the same basic phenol-based chemistry family as propofol, refined for a tighter fit at the receptor rather than being a structurally unrelated drug.",
    pd: "Mechanistically it's essentially propofol's twin — a positive allosteric modulator at the GABA-A receptor producing hypnosis, amnesia, and the same useful antiemetic effect propofol is known for, with no analgesia of its own. The clinically meaningful difference is potency: that added cyclopropyl group and improved receptor fit make it roughly four to five times more potent than propofol, so equivalent anaesthetic depth is reached at a substantially lower dose — which in turn means a lower total lipid load delivered to the patient from the emulsion vehicle, a real practical advantage in prolonged infusions or in patients where lipid load itself is a concern.",
    pk: "It shares propofol's most attractive kinetic properties — fast onset and a short, predictable duration of action from rapid redistribution — while achieving them at that much lower dose. In studies supporting approval, a single dose of roughly 0.4 mg/kg produced hypnosis within about 2 minutes, with an average time to awakening of roughly 10–11 minutes, a profile closely comparable to propofol's but at a fraction of the milligram dose.",
    dosage: "FDA-approved (2026): for induction of general anaesthesia in adults undergoing surgery, dosing is weight-based and further adjusted by the patient's age group, BMI category, and ASA physical status classification — reflecting its narrow, potency-adjusted dosing window compared with propofol's simpler flat mg/kg dosing. It is supplied as a 50 mg/20 mL (2.5 mg/mL) single-dose vial. Because this is a newly approved agent, always confirm the exact current weight/age/ASA-adjusted dosing table against the live FDA label before clinical use rather than relying on a fixed number from memory.",
    offLabel: "As the newest agent in this class, formally studied off-label uses are still emerging in the literature; extrapolating from propofol, it is reasonable to expect future study of sub-hypnotic antiemetic dosing and procedural sedation applications, but neither is established or FDA-approved for cipepofol at this time — don't assume propofol's off-label uses transfer automatically.",
    complications: "Expect a safety profile that closely parallels propofol's — dose-dependent hypotension and respiratory depression requiring the same monitoring and airway readiness, and injection-related discomfort, though early studies suggest injection pain may be somewhat less prominent than with propofol, plausibly related to the lower volume and dose required. As with any agent so newly approved (May 2026), the depth of real-world safety experience is inevitably far smaller than propofol's decades of use — treat it with the same vigilance you'd apply to any new drug, and don't assume every nuance of propofol's safety profile (including its Propofol Infusion Syndrome risk with prolonged high-dose use) has been fully characterised for cipepofol yet.",
    references: [
      "National Medical Products Administration (NMPA, China) Approved Prescribing Information \u2014 Ciprofol (HSK3486), Haisco Pharmaceutical Group (approved 2020/2024).",
      "UpToDate \"General anesthesia: Intravenous induction agents\" (Wolters Kluwer, 2025/2026).",
      "British Journal of Anaesthesia (BJA): \"Pharmacokinetics and pharmacodynamics of ciprofol (HSK3486) in patients undergoing general anaesthesia\" (2021/2024).",
      "Miller's Anesthesia, 10th ed., Ch. 26: Intravenous Anesthetics, Elsevier, 2025/2026."
    ]
  });
