  addDrug({
    id: "lidocaine", cat: "local", name: "Lidocaine", brand: "Xylocaine",
    classification: "Aminoamide • Intermediate-acting local anaesthetic (fast onset, hepatic CYP1A2/3A4)",
    tagline: "The prototype amide local anaesthetic — the one every other local gets compared to, and a drug in its own right IV",
    tags: ["Amide LA", "Class Ib antiarrhythmic"],
    source: SRC.fdaUpToDate("Xylocaine (lidocaine hydrochloride) injection label", "Fresenius Kabi/generic"),
    structure: "Lidocaine is the prototype amide local anaesthetic — an amide linkage joins an aromatic, lipid-loving ring to a hydrophilic tertiary amine tail through an intermediate alkyl chain. That amide linkage is the key structural fact to remember about this whole drug family: amide local anaesthetics are broken down by the liver, which is exactly why they behave so differently in hepatic disease compared with the ester local anaesthetics, which are instead hydrolysed by plasma cholinesterase.",
    pd: "It works by blocking voltage-gated sodium channels from inside the nerve cell membrane — the drug has to cross the membrane in its uncharged, lipid-soluble form first, then re-ionise inside the cell to actually plug the channel from within. Block sodium influx and you block action-potential propagation, which is the whole basis of local anaesthesia, whether that's a peripheral nerve block, a spinal, or simple infiltration. Smaller, less myelinated fibres are generally blocked first and at lower concentrations, though the classic teaching that this follows fibre size in a clean, simple order has real, well-documented exceptions. Beyond its use as a local anaesthetic, lidocaine also blocks cardiac sodium channels — the basis of its separate use as a Class Ib antiarrhythmic — and given IV, it has genuine systemic analgesic and anti-hyperalgesic effects independent of any nerve block at the injection site.",
    pk: "Onset is intermediate, around 2–5 minutes for infiltration or most blocks, with an intermediate duration of roughly 1–2 hours plain, longer with epinephrine added. It's broken down by hepatic CYP3A4/1A2 into active metabolites, including monoethylglycinexylidide (MEGX) — which itself contributes to systemic toxicity risk if a prolonged high-dose infusion or hepatic impairment lets it accumulate — with renal excretion of the metabolites.",
    dosage: "FDA-approved & Society Guidelines: 1) Infiltration & Peripheral Nerve Block: US FDA maximum recommended single dose is 4.5 mg/kg plain (maximum 300 mg) and 7.0 mg/kg with epinephrine 1:200,000 (maximum 500 mg). UK / European Association of Anaesthesiology guideline maximum is more conservative: 3.0 mg/kg plain (maximum 200 mg) and 7.0 mg/kg with epinephrine (maximum 500 mg). 2) IV Systemic Multimodal Analgesia (ERAS Protocols): Initial IV loading bolus of 1.5 mg/kg IV infused over 10 to 15 minutes (calculated on Ideal Body Weight), followed by a continuous infusion of 1.5 to 2.0 mg/kg/h (ideal body weight), discontinued at wound closure or continued in PACU/ward up to 24–48 hours with serum level monitoring (target therapeutic range 1.5–5.0 mcg/mL; toxicity > 5 mcg/mL). 3) IV Antiarrhythmic (Class Ib): 1 to 1.5 mg/kg IV bolus, repeatable at 0.5–0.75 mg/kg every 5–10 minutes (max total 3 mg/kg), followed by 1 to 4 mg/min continuous infusion. 4) IV Regional Anaesthesia (Bier Block): 3 mg/kg plain (approx 40–50 mL of 0.5% plain preservative-free solution). 5) Spinal (Intrathecal): Hyperbaric 5% in 7.5% dextrose at 1.0–1.5 mL (50–75 mg) — though largely replaced by bupivacaine/chloroprocaine due to transient neurologic symptoms (TNS).",
    offLabel: "An IV lidocaine infusion is a well-studied, widely adopted component of multimodal perioperative analgesia, particularly in abdominal surgery — several trials show it reduces opioid consumption, pain scores, and speeds return of bowel function, making it a genuine ERAS-pathway tool even though it's off-label relative to its approved local-anaesthetic and antiarrhythmic indications. Topical or nebulised lidocaine is also used to suppress cough during awake airway procedures. It's also the standard agent for IV regional anaesthesia (Bier block) — 0.5% plain lidocaine, roughly 3 mg/kg (about 40–50 mL in an average adult arm), a well-established technique that nonetheless sits outside the core label's enumerated routes.",
    complications: "Local anaesthetic systemic toxicity (LAST) from inadvertent intravascular injection or simply too large a total dose is the complication to know cold for this entire drug class — early CNS signs (perioral numbness, tinnitus, a metallic taste, agitation) progress to seizures, then cardiovascular signs (arrhythmia, conduction block, collapse), managed following the ASRA LAST checklist: airway management, seizure control, and IV lipid emulsion ('lipid rescue') if cardiovascular toxicity develops. Methaemoglobinaemia is more classically linked to prilocaine and benzocaine, but has been reported with lidocaine too. High-concentration intrathecal lidocaine (historically 5% hyperbaric) has been linked to transient neurologic symptoms, which is part of why it's now used less for spinal anaesthesia than it once was. Genuine allergic reactions are rare with amide local anaesthetics (far more common with esters, related to their PABA metabolite), but can still occur, sometimes tied to preservatives in the formulation rather than the drug itself.",
    references: [
      "FDA Prescribing Information — Xylocaine (lidocaine hydrochloride injection, Fresenius Kabi/AstraZeneca); UpToDate (2025/2026); Miller's Anesthesia, 10th ed., Ch. 34.",
      "Neal JM, et al. American Society of Regional Anesthesia and Pain Medicine Local Anesthetic Systemic Toxicity checklist: 2020 version. Reg Anesth Pain Med (2021).",
      "Beaussier M, et al. Perioperative Use of Intravenous Lidocaine: An Update on the Pharmacokinetics, Efficacy, and Safety. Drugs (2018)."
    ]
  });

  addDrug({
    id: "bupivacaine", cat: "local", name: "Bupivacaine", brand: "Marcaine / Sensorcaine (liposomal: Exparel)",
    classification: "Aminoamide • Long-acting local anaesthetic (high protein binding, cardiotoxicity risk)",
    tagline: "Long-acting and high-potency — and the local anaesthetic most likely to stop a heart if it gets into a vein by accident",
    tags: ["Amide LA", "Long-acting"],
    source: SRC.fdaUpToDate("Marcaine (bupivacaine hydrochloride) injection label; Exparel (bupivacaine liposome injectable suspension) label", "Pfizer (Marcaine)/Pacira (Exparel)"),
    structure: "Bupivacaine is an amide local anaesthetic, a butyl-substituted analogue of mepivacaine — that longer alkyl chain on the piperidine nitrogen is what increases its lipid solubility, potency and duration compared with mepivacaine or lidocaine, but it's also exactly what makes it more cardiotoxic. It's marketed both as the standard hydrochloride solution and as a liposomal extended-release suspension (Exparel) built for prolonged single-injection analgesia.",
    pd: "It blocks sodium channels the same way every amide local anaesthetic does, but with a pharmacological quirk that genuinely matters: it binds to and dissociates from cardiac sodium channels much more slowly than lidocaine does — sometimes described as 'fast-in, slow-out' kinetics. That slow dissociation is exactly why bupivacaine's cardiotoxicity is disproportionately severe relative to its CNS-toxicity threshold, compared with other amide local anaesthetics: cardiac arrest can occur with little or no preceding CNS warning, and resuscitation from bupivacaine-induced arrest is notoriously difficult without lipid emulsion therapy specifically.",
    pk: "Onset is slower than lidocaine's, 5–10 minutes, but duration is genuinely long — 4–8 hours plain, longer with epinephrine, and the liposomal formulation (Exparel) is built for sustained release over as long as 72–96 hours. It's metabolised hepatically by amide-metabolising CYP3A4/1A2, cleared renally, and highly protein-bound — which matters because toxicity risk rises in states of low protein binding capacity, such as hypoproteinaemia.",
    dosage: "FDA-approved (Marcaine): 1) Infiltration & Peripheral Nerve Block: Maximum single safe dose is 175 mg plain (approx 2.0 to 2.5 mg/kg) and 225 mg with epinephrine 1:200,000 (approx 3.0 mg/kg). Absolute cumulative 24-hour maximum ceiling is 400 mg. 2) Spinal (Intrathecal) Anaesthesia: Hyperbaric 0.75% in 8.25% dextrose (Marcaine Spinal): 7.5 to 15 mg (1.0 to 2.0 mL), dosed per required level (7.5–10 mg for lower extremity/perineal T10 block; 12–15 mg for caesarean section T4 block). Isobaric 0.5% plain: 10 to 15 mg (2.0 to 3.0 mL). 3) Epidural Anaesthesia: 0.25% to 0.5% solutions (10–20 mL; 25–100 mg). FDA Boxed Warning: 0.75% plain bupivacaine is strictly contraindicated for obstetric epidural anaesthesia due to fatal refractory cardiac arrest. 4) Liposomal Bupivacaine (Exparel): Single-dose surgical site infiltration up to 266 mg (20 mL of 1.3%), expanding release over 72–96 hours.",
    offLabel: "Lower-concentration plain bupivacaine (roughly 0.0625–0.125%) is standard practice for labour epidural analgesia, chosen specifically to preserve motor function while still providing good sensory analgesia — a well-established technique built on the drug's dose-dependent differential block.",
    complications: "Local anaesthetic systemic toxicity carries a disproportionately severe cardiotoxic signature with bupivacaine compared with other local anaesthetics — ventricular arrhythmias and cardiac arrest can appear abruptly and can be genuinely refractory to standard ACLS without prompt IV lipid emulsion therapy, a fact that directly drove the widespread adoption of lipid rescue protocols in the first place. The FDA label specifically warns against using the 0.75% concentration in obstetric epidural anaesthesia, following reports of cardiac arrest after accidental intravascular injection at that particular concentration. Chondrolysis has been reported with continuous intra-articular infusion, which the label specifically warns against. Methaemoglobinaemia is not a typical bupivacaine effect — that belongs more to the esters and to prilocaine.",
    references: [
      "FDA Prescribing Information — Marcaine (bupivacaine hydrochloride injection, Hospira/Pfizer); Exparel (Pacira); UpToDate (2025/2026); Miller's Anesthesia, 10th ed., Ch. 34.",
      "ASRA-PM Practice Advisory on Local Anesthetic Systemic Toxicity (LAST) (Reg Anesth Pain Med 2021).",
      "Stoelting's Pharmacology & Physiology in Anesthetic Practice, 5th ed."
    ]
  });

  addDrug({
    id: "ropivacaine", cat: "local", name: "Ropivacaine", brand: "Naropin",
    classification: "Aminoamide • Long-acting local anaesthetic (pure S-enantiomer, differential sensory block)",
    tagline: "Built as a deliberately safer bupivacaine — a single enantiomer with a genuinely wider cardiac safety margin",
    tags: ["Amide LA", "Long-acting", "Single enantiomer"],
    source: SRC.fdaUpToDate("Naropin (ropivacaine hydrochloride) injection label", "originally AstraZeneca/generic"),
    structure: "Ropivacaine is structurally close to bupivacaine and mepivacaine — a propyl-substituted piperidine analogue in the same amide family — but with one deliberate design choice: it's manufactured and marketed as a single, pure S(-)-enantiomer, rather than as a racemic mixture the way bupivacaine is. That's not a marketing detail — the S-enantiomer genuinely binds cardiac sodium channels less avidly and is inherently less cardiotoxic than the R-enantiomer, which is exactly the rationale behind the whole molecule.",
    pd: "It blocks sodium channels through the same core mechanism as any amide local anaesthetic. Clinically, at lower concentrations it shows a useful degree of differential sensory-over-motor block compared with bupivacaine — genuinely helpful for labour analgesia or ambulatory regional techniques where you want good sensory coverage without heavy motor block. And because of that single-enantiomer design, it has a meaningfully wider margin between the dose that causes CNS toxicity and the dose that causes cardiotoxicity than bupivacaine does — a real safety advantage if accidental intravascular injection happens, though it's important to be clear this margin is wider, not zero; ropivacaine toxicity is still a genuine emergency.",
    pk: "Onset (roughly 10–15 minutes) and duration (4–8 hours, depending on dose and concentration) are broadly similar to bupivacaine's. It's metabolised mainly by hepatic CYP1A2, with a CYP3A4 contribution too, cleared renally. It's slightly less lipid-soluble and slightly less potent than bupivacaine at equal concentrations, which is part of why higher concentrations of ropivacaine are typically used to achieve a comparable density of block.",
    dosage: "FDA-approved (Naropin): 1) Peripheral Nerve Block & Infiltration: Maximum recommended single dose is 225 to 250 mg plain (up to 300 mg for major plexus block), corresponding to approximately 3.0 mg/kg (not 4 mg/kg). Note: Epinephrine is rarely added because ropivacaine possesses intrinsic vascular smooth muscle vasoconstriction. 2) Epidural Surgical Anaesthesia: 0.5% to 1.0% solution (15–25 mL; 75–200 mg), with onset 10–15 minutes and duration 3–6 hours. 3) Epidural Labour Analgesia: 0.2% solution as a continuous infusion (6 to 14 mL/h) or programmed intermittent boluses. 4) Off-Label Spinal (Intrathecal): 15 to 25 mg (3–5 mL of 0.5%), requiring a higher milligram dose than bupivacaine for equivalent dermatomal density.",
    offLabel: "Continuous peripheral nerve catheter infusions for outpatient postoperative analgesia, at low concentrations (roughly 0.1–0.2%), extend somewhat beyond some of the originally studied dosing scenarios but are widely supported in the regional anaesthesia literature and are broadly consistent with its labelled peripheral-block use. Intrathecal ropivacaine, though not FDA-labelled for that route in the US, is used off-label — more established outside the US — for spinal anaesthesia at roughly 15–25 mg (3–5 mL of 0.5%), generally needing a higher milligram dose than bupivacaine for an equivalent block.",
    complications: "Local anaesthetic systemic toxicity remains possible, though the threshold and severity for cardiotoxicity are generally more favourable than bupivacaine's at equivalent doses — still managed with the same ASRA LAST protocol, lipid emulsion included, if cardiovascular toxicity occurs. CNS toxicity (seizures) can still occur with an excessive dose or accidental intravascular injection. Hypotension and bradycardia can follow a high neuraxial block from sympathetic blockade, exactly as with any neuraxial local anaesthetic. Motor block still occurs at higher concentrations and can delay ambulation after a regional technique if the dose isn't chosen carefully for the goal at hand.",
    references: [
      "FDA Prescribing Information — Naropin (ropivacaine hydrochloride injection, AstraZeneca/Fresenius Kabi); UpToDate (2025/2026); Miller's Anesthesia, 10th ed., Ch. 34.",
      "ASRA-PM Local Anesthetic Safety Guidelines (2021).",
      "Stoelting's Pharmacology & Physiology in Anesthetic Practice, 5th ed."
    ]
  });

  addDrug({
    id: "chloroprocaine", cat: "local", name: "Chloroprocaine", brand: "Nesacaine",
    classification: "Aminoester • Ultra-short-acting local anaesthetic (rapid plasma pseudocholinesterase clearance)",
    tagline: "The fastest-clearing local anaesthetic in clinical use — metabolised in the blood itself, not the liver",
    tags: ["Ester LA", "Rapid onset/offset"],
    source: SRC.fdaUpToDate("Nesacaine (chloroprocaine hydrochloride) injection label", "Sintetica/generic"),
    structure: "Chloroprocaine is an ester local anaesthetic — a chlorinated derivative of procaine — and that ester linkage, rather than the amide linkage lidocaine, bupivacaine and ropivacaine all share, is the single most important structural fact about it. Ester linkages are hydrolysed by plasma (pseudo)cholinesterase, which is exactly what gives chloroprocaine the fastest systemic clearance of any local anaesthetic in clinical use.",
    pd: "It blocks sodium channels through the same basic mechanism as every local anaesthetic here. What genuinely distinguishes it is that this ester structure means its metabolism doesn't depend on the liver at all — and its plasma hydrolysis is so rapid (a half-life of only around 20 to 45 seconds in adults) that both its short duration of action and its comparatively wide safety margin against systemic toxicity, even with fairly large epidural doses, follow directly from that single structural feature.",
    pk: "Onset is fast, roughly 6–12 minutes epidurally, with a short duration — 30 to 60 minutes plain, longer with epinephrine added. It's hydrolysed almost entirely by plasma cholinesterase, producing essentially no hepatic or renal clearance burden. One breakdown product is para-aminobenzoic acid (PABA), which is implicated in the genuinely higher rate of allergic reactions seen with ester local anaesthetics as a class, compared with the amides.",
    dosage: "FDA-approved (Nesacaine / Clorotekal): 1) Surgical Epidural Anaesthesia: 2% or 3% solution (15–25 mL; 300–750 mg). Maximum recommended single dose is 800 mg plain (up to 11 mg/kg) and 1000 mg with epinephrine 1:200,000 (up to 14 mg/kg). The exceptionally high single-dose tolerance is directly enabled by rapid vascular hydrolysis by plasma pseudocholinesterase (t1/2 20–45 seconds). 2) Emergency Caesarean Epidural Top-Up: 15 to 20 mL of 3% chloroprocaine with sodium bicarbonate (1 mL 8.4% bicarb per 10 mL chloroprocaine) provides dense surgical block within 3 to 5 minutes. 3) Ambulatory Spinal (Intrathecal): 30 to 50 mg (2–3 mL of preservative-free 2% or 1% solution, e.g. Clorotekal), providing reliable surgical block with complete motor recovery in 60 to 90 minutes.",
    offLabel: "It's a go-to for rapid emergency conversion of an existing labour epidural to surgical anaesthesia for an urgent caesarean delivery, exploiting its fast onset when speed genuinely matters. Preservative-free chloroprocaine given intrathecally for ambulatory or short-duration spinal anaesthesia is an increasingly supported off-label practice — typically 30–50 mg (2–3 mL of 2–3%) — valued for its fast offset in same-day surgery, though formal FDA labelling for the intrathecal route remains limited.",
    complications: "Neurotoxicity — arachnoiditis, prolonged neurologic deficit — reported historically with large-volume intrathecal use of earlier formulations was subsequently attributed largely to the antioxidant sodium bisulfite and the low pH of those older preparations, not to chloroprocaine itself; modern preservative-free formulations have a substantially better track record for off-label intrathecal use, though caution and careful practice remain warranted. Allergic and hypersensitivity reactions are more common with ester local anaesthetics generally, because of the PABA metabolite — genuinely important to remember in a patient who reports a vague 'local anaesthetic allergy,' since that history is much more often ester-related than amide-related. There are also scattered reports that it can antagonise the analgesic effect of subsequently given epidural opioids or local anaesthetics, an interaction that's recognised but not fully understood, most often noted with epidural morphine.",
    references: [
      "FDA Prescribing Information — Nesacaine (chloroprocaine hydrochloride injection, Sintetica/generic); Clorotekal (preservative-free spinal chloroprocaine); UpToDate (2025/2026).",
      "Miller's Anesthesia, 10th ed., Ch. 34; Stoelting's Pharmacology & Physiology in Anesthetic Practice, 5th ed.",
      "Goldblum E, Atchabahian A. The use of chloroprocaine for spinal anaesthesia. Acta Anaesthesiol Scand (2013)."
    ]
  });

  addDrug({
    id: "mepivacaine", cat: "local", name: "Mepivacaine", brand: "Polocaine / Carbocaine",
    classification: "Aminoamide • Intermediate-acting local anaesthetic (minimal intrinsic vasodilation)",
    tagline: "Similar to lidocaine but with less vasodilation — a plain solution that still lasts a useful while without epinephrine",
    tags: ["Amide LA", "Intermediate duration"],
    source: SRC.fdaUpToDate("Polocaine (mepivacaine hydrochloride) injection label", "Hospira/generic"),
    structure: "Mepivacaine is an amide local anaesthetic in the same pipecoloxylidide chemical family as bupivacaine and ropivacaine — a methyl-substituted piperidine analogue — but with a shorter alkyl substituent than either of them, giving it intermediate potency and duration, closer to lidocaine's than to the longer-acting agents.",
    pd: "It blocks sodium channels through the standard amide local anaesthetic mechanism. The one genuinely distinguishing feature worth remembering is that it causes less intrinsic vasodilation than lidocaine, which gives it a somewhat longer duration even without epinephrine added — a real practical advantage whenever you want a reasonably long plain (epinephrine-free) block, whether for obstetric reasons or simply because epinephrine isn't wanted for that particular case.",
    pk: "Onset (roughly 3–5 minutes) and duration (1.5–3 hours plain, longer with epinephrine or in a slower-turnover tissue plane) sit intermediate between lidocaine and the longer-acting bupivacaine/ropivacaine. It's cleared by hepatic amide metabolism and renal excretion. One detail worth knowing specifically for obstetric use: it has a notably slower placental clearance than some other amides, which historically drove caution about neonatal accumulation with maternal epidural mepivacaine — a real factor in choosing which local anaesthetic to use in labour.",
    dosage: "FDA-approved (Carbocaine / Polocaine): 1) Infiltration & Peripheral Nerve Block: 1% to 2% solutions. Maximum single recommended dose is 400 mg plain (approx 4.5 to 5.0 mg/kg) and 500 mg with epinephrine 1:200,000 (approx 7.0 mg/kg). Absolute cumulative 24-hour maximum ceiling is 1000 mg. 2) Epidural Anaesthesia: 1% to 2% solution (15–25 mL; 150–400 mg). 3) Off-Label Ambulatory Spinal Anaesthesia: 45 to 60 mg (1.5–2 mL of 3% or 3–4 mL of 1.5%), favored for intermediate-duration day-case surgery with faster motor recovery and significantly lower risk of transient neurologic symptoms (TNS) than lidocaine.",
    offLabel: "It's a genuinely popular choice for peripheral nerve blocks in ambulatory or outpatient surgery specifically because its intermediate duration gives adequate surgical anaesthesia while still allowing more predictable, earlier resolution of motor block than bupivacaine or ropivacaine would — useful when same-day discharge depends on the patient regaining function promptly. This is really a practice pattern within its labelled peripheral-block indications rather than a distinct off-label use in the strict sense. Intrathecal mepivacaine (isobaric 1.5% or hyperbaric 4%), though not FDA-labelled for that route in the US, is used off-label — more established in Canada and parts of Europe — for short ambulatory spinal anaesthesia, typically 45–60 mg, valued for its comparatively fast motor recovery.",
    complications: "Local anaesthetic systemic toxicity applies as with any amide, managed through the standard ASRA LAST protocol. Its comparatively longer neonatal elimination half-life historically raised concern about fetal or neonatal accumulation with maternal epidural use, which is part of why it's used more selectively in contemporary obstetric practice than lidocaine, bupivacaine or ropivacaine. Methaemoglobinaemia isn't a typical mepivacaine effect. Overall its safety profile is broadly similar in kind to lidocaine's, differing mainly in its longer plain duration and lower intrinsic vasoactivity.",
    references: [
      "FDA Prescribing Information — Carbocaine / Polocaine (mepivacaine hydrochloride injection, Hospira/Pfizer); UpToDate (2025/2026); Miller's Anesthesia, 10th ed., Ch. 34.",
      "ASRA-PM Practice Advisory on Local Anesthetic Systemic Toxicity (2021).",
      "Stoelting's Pharmacology & Physiology in Anesthetic Practice, 5th ed."
    ]
  });

