  addDrug({
    id: "oxytocin", cat: "pregnancy", name: "Oxytocin", brand: "Pitocin / Syntocinon",
    classification: "Synthetic nonapeptide uterotonic • Gq-coupled oxytocin receptor agonist",
    tagline: "First-line uterotonic for labour induction and PPH prophylaxis — rapid onset with dose-dependent vasodilation",
    tags: ["Uterotonic", "First-line PPH", "Nonapeptide"],
    source: "FDA Prescribing Information — Pitocin (oxytocin injection, Par Pharmaceutical); ACOG Practice Bulletin No. 183 (Postpartum Hemorrhage); RCOG Green-top Guideline No. 52; Miller's Anesthesia, 10th ed., Ch. 69 (Obstetric Anesthesia).",
    structure: "Oxytocin is a cyclic nonapeptide neurohypophysial hormone (molecular formula C43H66N12O12S2) composed of nine amino acids: Cys-Tyr-Ile-Gln-Asn-Cys-Pro-Leu-Gly-NH2. A single intramolecular disulfide bridge links cysteine residues at positions 1 and 6, forming a 20-membered ring with a tripeptide tail (Pro-Leu-Gly-NH2) at the C-terminus. The intact disulfide ring and C-terminal amide are essential for high-affinity binding and agonist activation at myometrial oxytocin receptors.",
    pd: "It selectively stimulates uterine oxytocin receptors (OTR, Gq-protein coupled), activating phospholipase C and generating inositol trisphosphate (IP3) and diacylglycerol (DAG), which mobilises intracellular calcium from the sarcoplasmic reticulum and opens voltage-gated calcium channels. This produces rhythmic, coordinated uterine smooth muscle contractions that increase in frequency and amplitude, transitioning to sustained tetanic contractions at higher infusion rates. Uterine sensitivity increases markedly throughout gestation, peaking at term due to estrogen-induced upregulation of OTR density (up to 300-fold). In peripheral vascular smooth muscle, rapid intravenous bolus doses stimulate endothelial nitric oxide release, triggering transient systemic vasodilation, significant acute hypotension, flushing, and compensatory baroreceptor-mediated tachycardia.",
    pk: "Following intravenous administration, onset of uterine contraction is almost immediate (within 1 minute), while intramuscular injection produces onset in 3–5 minutes. Plasma half-life is very short (roughly 3–5 minutes), driven by rapid degradation by circulating hepatic and placental aminopeptidase (oxytocinase) and renal clearance. Uterine effect persists for roughly 20–30 minutes after stopping an IV infusion, necessitating continuous infusion to maintain tone after delivery. Negligible cross-placental transfer occurs in clinical doses due to enzymatic degradation.",
    dosage: "FDA-approved: for active management of the 3rd stage of labour and prevention of postpartum haemorrhage (PPH), 10 units IM following delivery of the anterior shoulder or infant, or 10–40 units infused in 1000 mL non-hydrating IV fluid (e.g. normal saline or lactated Ringer's) at 20–40 mU/min (roughly 125–250 mL/h of a 20 units/L solution), titrated to uterine tone. For treatment of established PPH, 10–40 units in 1000 mL crystalloid infused rapidly, or a controlled IV bolus of 3–5 units given slowly over 1–2 minutes (following modern obstetric anaesthesia 'Rule of Threes' protocols to prevent cardiovascular collapse; rapid IV push is contraindicated). For induction or augmentation of labour, IV micro-infusion beginning at 0.5–2 mU/min, titrated upward by 1–2 mU/min every 15–30 minutes until established labour is achieved (maximum 20 mU/min).",
    offLabel: "The 'Rule of Threes' protocol for elective caesarean delivery under neuraxial anaesthesia is widely endorsed across modern obstetric anaesthesia guidelines (SOAP/OAA): an initial slow IV bolus of 3 units over 30 seconds to 1 minute, repeated at 3-minute intervals for up to 3 total doses if uterine tone is inadequate, followed by a maintenance infusion of 3 units/h (or 10 units over 4 hours). This evidence-based regimen provides equivalent uterine tone compared with older high-dose boluses (5–10 units) while dramatically reducing hemodynamic instability, nausea, and chest tightness.",
    complications: "Rapid intravenous push causes severe peripheral vasodilation, acute maternal hypotension, profound reflex tachycardia, and potential coronary hypoperfusion, which can precipitate myocardial ischaemia or cardiac arrest in women with cardiac disease or hypovolemia. Because oxytocin shares structural homology with antidiuretic hormone (arginine vasopressin), prolonged continuous infusions at high doses (> 20 mU/min) with hypotonic fluids (e.g. 5% dextrose) can cause severe water intoxication, maternal hyponatremia, cerebral oedema, and seizures. Uterine hyperstimulation, tachysystole (> 5 contractions in 10 minutes), or uterine rupture can occur during labour induction, particularly in scarred uteruses or trial of labour after caesarean (TOLAC). Mild nausea, vomiting, and headache are common transient side effects.",
    references: [
      "FDA-Approved Prescribing Information \u2014 Pitocin (oxytocin injection, USP), Par Pharmaceutical / Endo; US FDA.",
      "UpToDate \"Drug Information: Oxytocin\" (Wolters Kluwer, 2025/2026).",
      "ACOG Practice Bulletin No. 183: Postpartum Hemorrhage (Obstet Gynecol, reaffirmed 2023/2025).",
      "Society for Obstetric Anesthesia and Perinatology (SOAP) Consensus Statement on Uterotonic Agents (Anesth Analg, 2020/2024).",
      "Chestnut's Obstetric Anesthesia: Principles and Practice, 6th ed., Elsevier."
    ]
  });

  addDrug({
    id: "carbetocin", cat: "pregnancy", name: "Carbetocin", brand: "Pabal / Duratocin",
    classification: "Synthetic 1-deamino-1-carba oxytocin analogue • Long-acting oxytocin receptor agonist",
    tagline: "Long-acting synthetic oxytocin analogue — single-dose PPH prophylaxis with prolonged uterotonic action",
    tags: ["Uterotonic", "Long-acting", "PPH prophylaxis"],
    source: "WHO Recommendations: Uterotonics for the prevention of postpartum haemorrhage (2018/2023 update); SOGC Clinical Practice Guideline No. 433; Health Canada / MHRA Prescribing Information (Duratocin / Pabal); UpToDate 'Management of the third stage of labor' (2025).",
    structure: "Carbetocin is a synthetic 1-deamino-1-carba-2-O-methyltyrosine analogue of human oxytocin (molecular formula C45H69N11O12S). Two critical chemical modifications distinguish it from native oxytocin: the N-terminal amino group is removed (deamino) and the disulfide bond (-S-S-) is replaced by a stable thioether/carba linkage (-CH2-CH2-S-), alongside O-methylation of the tyrosine residue at position 2. These structural alterations confer high resistance to aminopeptidase and oxytocinase cleavage, dramatically prolonging its biological half-life while retaining full agonist affinity at myometrial oxytocin receptors.",
    pd: "It binds selectively and with high affinity to uterine oxytocin receptors (OTR), stimulating the Gq-PLC-IP3 signalling cascade to elevate intracellular calcium and elicit rhythmic, coordinated uterine contractions that rapidly transition to firm, sustained basal myometrial tone. In clinical trials, single-dose carbetocin produces uterine tone comparable to a multi-hour oxytocin infusion, with lower requirements for secondary rescue uterotonics in caesarean deliveries. Like oxytocin, rapid IV injection triggers transient vascular smooth muscle relaxation and mild nitric oxide-mediated vasodilation, but the hemodynamic drop is attenuated compared to rapid high-dose oxytocin.",
    pk: "Following IV administration, onset of strong uterine contractions occurs within 1.2 minutes, lasting approximately 60 minutes for rhythmic contractions and up to 2 hours for tetanic tone. Intramuscular administration achieves peak uterine contraction in 3–5 minutes and sustained uterotonic effect for 2–3 hours. Its elimination half-life is approximately 40 minutes (roughly 4–10 times longer than oxytocin), with less than 1% excreted unchanged in urine; hepatic metabolism and non-oxytocinase enzymatic degradation predominate.",
    dosage: "FDA/Health Canada/MHRA-approved: for the prevention of uterine atony and postpartum haemorrhage following caesarean section under regional anaesthesia, a single dose of 100 mcg (1.0 mL) administered slowly over 1 minute by intravenous injection immediately following delivery of the infant and placenta. In jurisdictions with vaginal delivery indications (WHO/Europe/Canada), a single dose of 100 mcg IM is administered following delivery of the anterior shoulder. It is intended strictly as a single dose; repeated administration is not recommended.",
    offLabel: "Carbetocin is endorsed by the World Health Organization (WHO) and international obstetric societies as an alternative to oxytocin for PPH prevention in settings where oxytocin cold-chain storage (2–8°C) cannot be reliably guaranteed, as heat-stable carbetocin formulations maintain potency for up to 36 months at 30°C. In high-risk obstetric surgeries (e.g. multiple gestation, large fibroids, polyhydramnios), single-dose carbetocin is widely used as a primary prophylactic agent to eliminate the need for prolonged 8–24 hour post-delivery IV oxytocin infusions.",
    complications: "The most common adverse effects include transient facial flushing, warmth, headache, nausea, abdominal cramping, and metallic taste. When administered too quickly as an IV bolus, transient hypotension and compensatory tachycardia can occur; it should be administered slowly over at least 60 seconds. It is strictly contraindicated prior to delivery of the infant (cannot be used for labour induction or augmentation due to risk of unremitting hypertonic uterine contractions and fetal asphyxia). It is contraindicated in patients with severe cardiovascular disease, hepatic/renal disease, or hypersensitivity to oxytocin.",
    references: [
      "European Medicines Agency (EMA) / UK MHRA Summary of Product Characteristics \u2014 Pabal (carbetocin 100 mcg/mL), Ferring Pharmaceuticals.",
      "UpToDate \"Management of the third stage of labor: Prophylactic uterotonics\" (Wolters Kluwer, 2025/2026).",
      "World Health Organization (WHO) Recommendations: Uterotonics for the prevention of postpartum haemorrhage (WHO Guidelines Approved by the Guidelines Review Committee, Geneva).",
      "Chestnut's Obstetric Anesthesia: Principles and Practice, 6th ed., Elsevier."
    ]
  });

  addDrug({
    id: "carboprost", cat: "pregnancy", name: "Carboprost (15-Methyl PGF2α)", brand: "Hemabate",
    classification: "Synthetic 15-methyl prostaglandin F2α analogue • Myometrial FP prostanoid receptor agonist",
    tagline: "Potent second-line prostaglandin uterotonic — essential rescue for refractory PPH, strictly contraindicated in asthma",
    tags: ["Uterotonic", "Second-line PPH", "Prostaglandin"],
    source: "FDA Prescribing Information — Hemabate (carboprost tromethamine injection, Pfizer); ACOG Practice Bulletin No. 183; RCOG Green-top Guideline No. 52; Miller's Anesthesia, 10th ed., Ch. 69.",
    structure: "Carboprost is a synthetic 15-methyl derivative of naturally occurring prostaglandin F2α (PGF2α), formulated as the tromethamine salt (molecular formula C21H36O5 for the free acid, C25H47NO8 for the tromethamine conjugate). The specific addition of a methyl group at carbon 15 prevents enzymatic oxidation and metabolic inactivation by 15-hydroxyprostaglandin dehydrogenase (15-OH-PGDH), significantly extending its duration of biological activity relative to endogenous PGF2α.",
    pd: "It is a potent agonist at prostanoid FP receptors located in myometrial smooth muscle. Binding triggers massive intracellular calcium influx via Gq-coupled protein cascades, producing intense, unyielding uterine contractions, marked myometrial tone, and mechanical compression of uterine spiral arteries to arrest postpartum haemorrhage. Beyond the uterus, carboprost vigorously stimulates bronchial smooth muscle (potent bronchoconstrictor), gastrointestinal longitudinal and circular smooth muscle (accelerating transit and hypersecretion), and pulmonary arterial vasculature (pulmonary vasoconstriction).",
    pk: "Following deep intramuscular injection, peak plasma concentrations (Cmax) are achieved in 15–30 minutes, with onset of increased uterine tone occurring within 10–15 minutes. Uterine contractions remain stimulated for 2 to 3 hours. It is cleared via oxidation of side chains and glucuronidation in the liver, with primary excretion via the kidneys; unlike native PGF2α, it is not rapidly inactivated on first pass through the pulmonary capillary bed.",
    dosage: "FDA-approved: for the treatment of refractory postpartum haemorrhage due to uterine atony that has failed to respond to oxytocin and ergometrine, an initial dose of 250 mcg (1.0 mL) administered by deep intramuscular (IM) injection. If uterine tone remains inadequate, repeat doses of 250 mcg IM may be given at intervals of 15 to 90 minutes. The maximum cumulative total dose is 2 mg (8 doses of 250 mcg). It must NEVER be administered intravenously due to the danger of lethal pulmonary hypertension, bronchospasm, and cardiovascular collapse. For second-trimester abortion, 250 mcg deep IM repeated every 1.5 to 3.5 hours depending on uterine response.",
    offLabel: "Direct intramyometrial injection (250 mcg into the uterine fundus during open caesarean delivery) is widely practiced by obstetric surgeons when uterine atony is refractory to IV oxytocin and IM injections. While not formally labelled as a distinct administration route in the package insert, extensive obstetric literature and society guidelines recognise intramyometrial administration as an accepted emergency rescue technique under direct surgical vision.",
    complications: "Severe bronchospasm and acute airway constriction can be life-threatening — carboprost is strictly CONTRAINDICATED in patients with active asthma or reactive airway disease. Gastrointestinal toxicity is virtually universal due to FP receptor activation on intestinal smooth muscle: profuse secretory diarrhoea occurs in up to 60–70% of patients, accompanied by severe nausea, retching, and vomiting (prophylactic antiemetics and antidiarrheals are routinely given). Pulmonary vasoconstriction can elevate pulmonary artery pressures and cause acute right heart strain and significant ventilation-perfusion (V/Q) mismatch with desaturation. Maternal pyrexia and fever (temperature spike > 1°C in up to 12% of patients) and peripheral hypertension from systemic vasoconstriction can also occur.",
    references: [
      "FDA-Approved Prescribing Information \u2014 Hemabate (carboprost tromethamine injection), Pfizer; US FDA.",
      "UpToDate \"Drug Information: Carboprost tromethamine\" (Wolters Kluwer, 2025/2026).",
      "ACOG Practice Bulletin No. 183: Postpartum Hemorrhage (Obstet Gynecol, reaffirmed 2023/2025).",
      "Chestnut's Obstetric Anesthesia: Principles and Practice, 6th ed., Elsevier."
    ]
  });

  addDrug({
    id: "methergine", cat: "pregnancy", name: "Methergine (Methylergometrine / Methylergonovine)", brand: "Methergine",
    classification: "Semi-synthetic ergot alkaloid derivative • Alpha-1 adrenergic and 5-HT2 receptor agonist",
    tagline: "Ergot alkaloid producing sustained tetanic uterine tone — strictly contraindicated in hypertension and pre-eclampsia",
    tags: ["Uterotonic", "Ergot alkaloid", "Second-line PPH"],
    source: "FDA Prescribing Information — Methergine (methylergonovine maleate injection/tablets, Novartis/Lupin); ACOG Practice Bulletin No. 183; RCOG Green-top Guideline No. 52; Miller's Anesthesia, 10th ed.",
    structure: "Methylergonovine (methylergometrine) is a semi-synthetic ergot alkaloid derivative of lysergic acid (molecular formula C20H25N3O2 as free base, C24H29N3O6 as the maleate salt). It is the N-[(2S)-1-hydroxybutan-2-yl] carboxamide derivative of methylergoline, featuring a tetracyclic indolo[4,3-fg]quinoline ring system. It differs from naturally occurring ergometrine by possessing an additional methyl group on the aliphatic side chain, which increases its potency and stability.",
    pd: "It acts as a partial agonist and antagonist at alpha-1 adrenergic, serotonergic (5-HT2), and dopaminergic receptors on vascular and uterine smooth muscle. In the gravid and postpartum uterus, it induces rapid, continuous, tetanic myometrial contractions and elevates basal resting tone, with subsequent rhythmic contractions superimposed. The resulting high-pressure mechanical compression of myometrial blood vessels provides rapid haemostasis at the placental bed. Concurrently, alpha-1 receptor stimulation in peripheral arterial and coronary vascular beds causes intense vasoconstriction, which can drive catastrophic surges in maternal blood pressure.",
    pk: "Following intramuscular injection, onset of strong uterine contraction is fast, occurring within 2–5 minutes; following oral tablets, onset is 5–10 minutes; IV onset is immediate (within 30–60 seconds). Bioavailability is approximately 60–78% after oral administration. Uterine tetany persists for roughly 45–90 minutes, with rhythmic contractions continuing for up to 3 hours. It is extensively metabolised in the liver by the cytochrome P450 3A4 (CYP3A4) pathway and eliminated primarily in bile and faeces, with an elimination half-life of 2–3 hours.",
    dosage: "FDA-approved: for the prevention and treatment of postpartum and postabortal haemorrhage due to uterine atony or subinvolution: 0.2 mg (1.0 mL) administered intramuscularly (IM) after delivery of the placenta (or anterior shoulder under specific protocol). The dose may be repeated every 2 to 4 hours as needed, up to a maximum of 5 doses (1.0 mg total). Following initial parenteral control of bleeding, oral dosing of 0.2 mg (one tablet) three or four times daily (every 6–8 hours) may be continued for up to 7 days for subinvolution. Intravenous administration is strongly discouraged and must be restricted only to life-threatening emergency situations, given slowly over not less than 60 seconds with continuous arterial pressure monitoring.",
    offLabel: "In emergency surgical scenarios where peripheral muscular access or IV access is compromised during laparotomy for PPH, intramyometrial injection (0.2 mg diluted in saline) is sometimes employed as an unlabelled rescue measure, though standard deep IM remains the gold-standard recommendation due to more predictable systemic absorption and reduced risk of acute intravascular bolusing.",
    complications: "Acute severe hypertension, hypertensive crisis, cerebral haemorrhage, and stroke are primary life-threatening risks — Methergine is strictly CONTRAINDICATED in patients with chronic hypertension, gestational hypertension, pre-eclampsia, eclampsia, or peripheral vascular disease. Coronary artery vasospasm, myocardial infarction, and ventricular arrhythmias have occurred, even in young women without pre-existing cardiac disease, particularly following rapid IV injection. Severe nausea, vomiting, dizziness, headache, and chest pressure are frequently reported. Concomitant use with potent CYP3A4 inhibitors (macrolide antibiotics, azole antifungals, protease inhibitors) is contraindicated due to danger of vasospastic ergotism and peripheral ischemia.",
    references: [
      "FDA-Approved Prescribing Information \u2014 Methergine (methylergonovine maleate injection / tablets), Novartis / ANI Pharmaceuticals; US FDA.",
      "UpToDate \"Drug Information: Methylergonovine\" (Wolters Kluwer, 2025/2026).",
      "ACOG Practice Bulletin No. 183: Postpartum Hemorrhage (Obstet Gynecol, reaffirmed 2023/2025).",
      "Chestnut's Obstetric Anesthesia: Principles and Practice, 6th ed., Elsevier."
    ]
  });

  addDrug({
    id: "misoprostol", cat: "pregnancy", name: "Misoprostol", brand: "Cytotec",
    classification: "Synthetic methyl ester prostaglandin E1 analogue • Myometrial EP2/EP3 receptor agonist",
    tagline: "Synthetic prostaglandin E1 analogue — temperature-stable uterotonic with versatile oral, sublingual, and rectal routes",
    tags: ["Uterotonic", "Prostaglandin E1", "PPH & Induction"],
    source: "WHO Guidelines for the Management of Postpartum Haemorrhage (2018/2023 update); ACOG Practice Bulletin No. 183; FIGO Guidelines: Prevention and treatment of PPH with misoprostol; FDA prescribing information (Cytotec — Pfizer); Miller's Anesthesia, 10th ed.",
    structure: "Misoprostol is a synthetic methyl ester analogue of naturally occurring prostaglandin E1 (PGE1), with chemical name methyl (E)-7-[(1R,2R,3R)-3-hydroxy-2-[(E)-4-hydroxy-4-methyloct-1-enyl]-5-oxocyclopentyl]heptanoate (molecular formula C22H38O5). Chemical modifications include a methyl group and tertiary hydroxyl at C16 alongside an esterified C1 carboxyl group; these changes protect the molecule against rapid enzymatic degradation by prostaglandin 15-hydroxydehydrogenase, conferring high oral bioavailability and room-temperature shelf stability without refrigeration.",
    pd: "It acts as a potent agonist at prostanoid EP2 and EP3 receptors on myometrial smooth muscle cells. EP3 receptor activation inhibits adenylate cyclase and opens receptor-operated calcium channels, triggering rapid intracellular calcium entry and vigorous myometrial contractions and tone. In the cervix, it promotes collagen degradation, rearrangement of extracellular ground substance, and cervical softening and dilation (ripening). Because it is not an FP-prostanoid (unlike PGF2α/carboprost), it does NOT cause bronchospasm and can be safely administered to asthmatic patients.",
    pk: "Following oral administration, misoprostol is rapidly and extensively absorbed, undergoing rapid de-esterification in the liver to its active metabolite, misoprostol acid. Absorption and onset vary considerably by route: sublingual administration provides the highest peak concentration (Cmax) and fastest onset (under 10 minutes), buccal administration achieves a prolonged plateau, and rectal administration yields a slower onset (20–30 minutes) with longer duration of action (up to 4 hours). Plasma protein binding of misoprostol acid is approximately 80–90%, and its elimination half-life is 20–40 minutes, with predominantly renal excretion.",
    dosage: "FDA-approved: originally labelled for the prevention of NSAID-induced gastric ulcers (100–200 mcg PO QID). Obstetric dosing is supported by FDA labelling (for termination) and WHO/FIGO/ACOG society guidelines: for the treatment of postpartum haemorrhage when injectable uterotonics are unavailable or have failed, 800 mcg administered sublingually (SL) or 800–1000 mcg administered rectally (PR) as a single dose. For PPH prevention in the absence of oxytocin (especially in community/low-resource settings), 400–600 mcg orally or sublingually immediately after delivery. For labour induction and cervical ripening at term: 25 mcg orally or vaginally (PV) every 3 to 6 hours, with continuous fetal heart rate and uterine monitoring.",
    offLabel: "While originally approved as a gastroprotective tablet, obstetric use of misoprostol for PPH prevention, PPH treatment, second-trimester termination, and management of early pregnancy failure (miscarriage: 800 mcg PV or SL) is universally recognised as standard of care across world obstetric practice (WHO Model List of Essential Medicines, ACOG, RCOG, FIGO). In modern obstetric anaesthesia, rectal administration of 800–1000 mcg at the conclusion of caesarean delivery in patients at high risk of delayed uterine atony is a widely utilised off-label adjuvant.",
    complications: "Shivering and intense rigors occur in up to 30–50% of patients receiving high sublingual or oral doses (e.g. 600–800 mcg), which can be distressing to the patient and interfere with postoperative monitoring. Transient hyperpyrexia and maternal fever (> 38°C in up to 30%, occasionally exceeding 40°C with high doses) can mimic chorioamnionitis or sepsis. Gastrointestinal symptoms including diarrhoea, abdominal cramping, and nausea/vomiting are common. When used for labour induction, uterine tachysystole and hyperstimulation can occur, carrying a serious risk of uterine rupture in women with a previous caesarean scar or uterine surgery (contraindicated for labour induction in trial of labour after caesarean).",
    references: [
      "FDA-Approved Prescribing Information \u2014 Cytotec (misoprostol tablets), Pfizer; US FDA.",
      "UpToDate \"Drug Information: Misoprostol\" (Wolters Kluwer, 2025/2026).",
      "ACOG Practice Bulletin No. 183: Postpartum Hemorrhage (Obstet Gynecol, reaffirmed 2023/2025).",
      "World Health Organization (WHO) Guidelines: Treatment of Postpartum Haemorrhage (Geneva).",
      "Chestnut's Obstetric Anesthesia: Principles and Practice, 6th ed., Elsevier."
    ]
  });

  
